                        HERALDS OF THE CEMÍ



    I should explain Sofía. She runs the tenant association’s research,
which means she’s the reason three buildings on this block are still
rent-stabilized. She once read my first apartment’s lease out loud at the
walk-through, in a normal voice, and the landlord changed the lock on
his own door. She can look at a system and see what it’s for.
    “Aperture Civic,” she said, no hello. “Registered in 2024. The web‐
site’s five months old, and beautiful. No employees on any record I can
find, which in my professional opinion is a flag the size of a building.”
    “Okay—”
    “Registered through three shells. I followed the paper until it
stopped being paper and started being a lawyer’s lunch.” She spun the
laptop around. “It ends at a holding company that also owns a youth
digital-wellness nonprofit, a music-licensing outfit, and a development
group that’s filed permits on four buildings on your block. Including
yours.”
    “Mine.”
    “The Ballroom. Ground floor, the arts space. Your mural.” Her finger
moved on the trackpad. “Eleven tabs. Manny owes me a dollar. He said
tabs.”
    On the screen was a pie chart. Manny was going to be unbearable.
    “Here’s what I can’t figure out,” she said. “Why would a real estate
company need your brain? The email, the permits, the same week, all
pointing at one date. Look at the date they gave you: June thirteenth,
Founders’ Night, at the Armory. They’ve got you on a clock.” She tilted
her head. “Now tell me what you were looking at when I walked in,
and don’t say nothing, because I know your nothing face and this isn’t
it.”
    She didn’t wait for an answer. She clicked and turned the screen. A
photo: a tall, gray, courteous man in a good coat at a ribbon-cutting, a
pair of oversized scissors in one hand and a small black notebook in the
other.
    “And here’s the only human being I can find,” she said. “Adrian
Vale, Director of Community Partnerships. He signed your email. He’s
in nine photos online, and he’s holding that notebook in every one.”
    I looked at the notebook for longer than I wanted to.



